WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:05.360
so we have the latest jobs numbers and once
again the latest jobs for it has missed expectations.

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:06.529
But I want to talk about it

00:00:06.529 --> 00:00:09.980
typically in order really to have a conversation.

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:15.190
Um, I want to make more of an effort to go
beyond just the latest numbers and to really

00:00:15.190 --> 00:00:19.769
make sure we're understanding the big issues
that are underlying these jobs numbers and

00:00:19.769 --> 00:00:24.130
the systemic economic problems that are going
to have to be solved if we're really going

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:29.769
to progress as a society and progress as an
economy for the sort of betterment and wellbeing

00:00:29.769 --> 00:00:33.420
of everybody in the country, on the planet,
whatever.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:38.040
So as a starting point, let's quickly deal
with the not at the numbers themselves.

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:39.840
New Jobs, again, have slowed down.

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:46.120
They fell short of expected numbers in May
only 75,000 jobs were added in May.

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:52.240
The expected number was 175,000 so number
one, it's really important to understand that

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:57.010
because more and more people live in the United
States over time and based on their ages,

00:00:57.010 --> 00:01:00.650
the number of working age people also fluctuates.

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:05.729
But as a really general idea, you need to
create some new jobs just to keep up with

00:01:05.729 --> 00:01:07.130
population growth.

00:01:07.130 --> 00:01:09.299
There are different estimates of what that
number is.

00:01:09.299 --> 00:01:11.929
I've seen as low as 80,000 jobs a month.

00:01:11.929 --> 00:01:17.389
I've seen as high as 120,000 jobs a month,
eight of complex and pretty widely contested

00:01:17.389 --> 00:01:22.780
number and I know no matter what number arrange
I give, some people are going to disagree

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:25.250
with me, but understand the concept.

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:32.170
If 75,000 new jobs were created and you need
80,000 or a hundred thousand or 120,000 just

00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:38.149
to account for population growth, we're falling
behind one way or the other.

00:01:38.149 --> 00:01:44.640
The only debate is how much are we falling
behind when we only create 75,000 jobs.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:49.869
Now the trendline is also a concern because
we got below expected numbers for several

00:01:49.869 --> 00:01:51.299
months in a row.

00:01:51.299 --> 00:01:55.609
The numbers from March were revised down from
the initial reports.

00:01:55.609 --> 00:02:00.679
March was initially reported at one 89,000
and then the numbers were fine tuned and we

00:02:00.679 --> 00:02:06.450
found out it was actually 153,000 new jobs
in March, which is in great April, was reported

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:09.060
as 263,000.

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:10.060
It was fine tuned.

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:13.750
And then it turns out it was actually only
224,000 new jobs.

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:19.210
So unemployment is at a historically low rate,
a new job.

00:02:19.210 --> 00:02:22.310
Numbers are falling short of expectations
regularly.

00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:29.310
Now payrolls have ticked up slightly, but
less than inflation, which is an important

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:31.030
metric to consider.

00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:37.340
So if we step back and we say, how is the
Trump jobs economy, it's more or less the

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:41.740
trend line that we saw under Obama with some
slow down.

00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:46.150
It's basically a very similar trajectory except
it is slowing down to some degree.

00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:48.700
Now how much of it is because of Trump?

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:49.700
Some but not all.

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:53.930
Uh, uh, some of what Donald Trump has done
is already certainly having an effect on the

00:02:53.930 --> 00:02:55.670
broader economic conditions.

00:02:55.670 --> 00:03:00.280
And then stepping back from that, there's
sort of like a broader geopolitical, a circumstance

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:05.350
that, um, exists not in a vacuum but sort
of on a continuum.

00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:10.450
And that has carried on from the Obama presidency
into the Trump presidency.

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:15.070
Now let's get to the really important stuff
because everything I've told you so far, it's

00:03:15.070 --> 00:03:21.850
important to know, but tracking these numbers
will not and does not address the real problems

00:03:21.850 --> 00:03:25.980
that we have in the United States around working
and labor and wages.

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:27.210
I'll give you an example.

00:03:27.210 --> 00:03:28.640
Here's a problem.

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:35.900
A ton of work in our economy is assigned no
economic value and is treated as such, which

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:37.540
has a whole number of different effects.

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:43.390
So if you stay home to raise kids or you take
care of elderly parents instead of working

00:03:43.390 --> 00:03:49.500
or you volunteer in work, that strengthens
our communities in ways that don't have a

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:54.010
specifically assigned economic value, but
obviously have value to the economy.

00:03:54.010 --> 00:03:59.920
If kids aren't raised in a good way to use
a really sort of vague general term.

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:05.430
If kids aren't raised in a way that makes
them a good members of our communities that

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:10.770
has an economic costs, they are more likely
to commit crime, which costs money in terms

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:14.000
of police and property damage and whatever
else.

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:22.150
So you can understand how raising kids properly
has an economic value, but it's hard to exactly

00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:23.811
assign a number to it.

00:04:23.811 --> 00:04:28.090
And if you can't do that in our economy, it's
effectively valued at zero.

00:04:28.090 --> 00:04:29.790
That's a major problem.

00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:35.430
That's not addressed by looking at did we
have 150,000 or 200,000 jobs created issue

00:04:35.430 --> 00:04:36.530
number two.

00:04:36.530 --> 00:04:43.290
Even when we look at wage growth, whether
we have 0.2% wage growth or 5% or we annualize

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:48.450
it to three, four or 5% wage growth or whatever,
and we look at the numbers over the last 40

00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:54.870
years, what you have to understand is that
the lion's share of the mean wage growth that

00:04:54.870 --> 00:05:00.010
has taken place over the last many decades
has gone disproportionately to the top.

00:05:00.010 --> 00:05:06.950
Almost all of the economic gains since 1980
have gone to the top half of earners rather

00:05:06.950 --> 00:05:09.320
than the bottom half of earners.

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:15.600
A ton of it has gone to the top 1% a crazy
disproportionate amount has gone to the top

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:24.820
0.1% so you can argue about Trump has oh 0.5%
wage growth and Obama had oh 0.4 or whatever.

00:05:24.820 --> 00:05:29.980
But if most of it goes to the rich, it's not
stimulating the economy as much as it could.

00:05:29.980 --> 00:05:35.640
It's making society more unequal, which increases
crime and all sorts of other that needs to

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:36.640
be solved.

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:45.030
So the point I'm trying to make to you is
that bragging about were up 0.2% in wages

00:05:45.030 --> 00:05:53.240
or the unemployment rate has ticked up or
down 0.1% or 150,000 versus 160,000 job creation.

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:57.500
It doesn't deal with these longterm systemic
problems that when members of Congress are

00:05:57.500 --> 00:06:01.520
reelected every two years when presidents
are reelected every four years and senators

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:08.860
are reelected every six, there is no sort
of of a, a baseline incentive to actually

00:06:08.860 --> 00:06:09.860
deal with that.

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:16.760
And by the way, the political battle over
the jobs reports doesn't touch inequality.

00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:22.350
It doesn't touch work that's valued at zero
by the economy and none of it deals with automation

00:06:22.350 --> 00:06:27.570
and technological unemployment and the fact
that we're going to have to rethink human

00:06:27.570 --> 00:06:29.260
relationships to work.

00:06:29.260 --> 00:06:35.919
There are a ton of ideas around how do we
think about a technological unemployment and

00:06:35.919 --> 00:06:38.580
automation and robotics and artificial intelligence.

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:41.110
We've heard about universal basic income.

00:06:41.110 --> 00:06:46.260
We've heard about a government guaranteed
jobs programs, negative income tax rates.

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:53.010
The most important question is going to be
who gets the gains from technology.

00:06:53.010 --> 00:06:58.340
If nothing changes in our economic system
and we know the answer, the huge corporations

00:06:58.340 --> 00:07:03.880
and the rich people who buy the robots and
the automation technology and the artificial

00:07:03.880 --> 00:07:07.800
intelligence infrastructures, they are going
to get the gains which is going to make the

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:09.680
country more unequal.

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:15.041
It's not going to do demand side stimulus,
meaning giving people money that they can

00:07:15.041 --> 00:07:21.030
spend, uh, imagine a company with a hundred
employees and the technology that lets them

00:07:21.030 --> 00:07:22.780
double productivity.

00:07:22.780 --> 00:07:25.650
There are lots of ways to distribute the gains.

00:07:25.650 --> 00:07:32.290
A pure capitalism solution might be the company
fires 50 people and they're able to keep producing

00:07:32.290 --> 00:07:36.780
the same amount of stuff with half the employees
because of this great technology, it's terrible

00:07:36.780 --> 00:07:37.790
for the economy.

00:07:37.790 --> 00:07:40.750
50 people are now out of a job.

00:07:40.750 --> 00:07:45.300
They don't have money with which they can
go and buy things from other companies.

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:51.979
So those other companies that want to be selling
stuff to those 50 people blues out and those

00:07:51.979 --> 00:07:53.990
other companies end up having to let people
go.

00:07:53.990 --> 00:07:54.990
So that's not good.

00:07:54.990 --> 00:08:00.540
Like a pure free market capitalism solution
for technological unemployment and automation

00:08:00.540 --> 00:08:02.389
technology is not good.

00:08:02.389 --> 00:08:08.509
The employer could also say, instead of firing
50 people, we'll keep all hundred people,

00:08:08.509 --> 00:08:13.870
but now with this new technology that doubles
productivity, each employee only has to work

00:08:13.870 --> 00:08:18.770
20 hours a week instead of 40 hours a week
and they can all keep making the same amount

00:08:18.770 --> 00:08:24.740
of money and the capitalists, so to speak,
the ownership, the business doesn't get any

00:08:24.740 --> 00:08:25.740
of the gains.

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:27.139
Now companies aren't going to do that.

00:08:27.139 --> 00:08:32.630
A fair solution would be that the company
gets a little bit of those games because they're

00:08:32.630 --> 00:08:34.729
buying the new technology.

00:08:34.729 --> 00:08:36.919
But the workers also get some of it.

00:08:36.919 --> 00:08:40.310
I don't know exactly what the numbers would
be, but that would be sort of a solution from

00:08:40.310 --> 00:08:42.450
the world of social democracy.

00:08:42.450 --> 00:08:48.100
But as you can see, there has to be regulation
in order to prevent the company from just

00:08:48.100 --> 00:08:52.380
taking all the gains and firing half of their
workforce.

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:55.780
And that's just one of the problems that needs
to be solved.

00:08:55.780 --> 00:09:03.310
So my message to you today is that we can't
take any politician seriously that isn't talking

00:09:03.310 --> 00:09:10.890
about the deeper issues, job creation numbers,
unemployment rates, wage growth, these ideas

00:09:10.890 --> 00:09:14.110
of we're going to bring back manufacturing.

00:09:14.110 --> 00:09:21.110
These are surface issues that if we solve,
are effectively bandaids in our economic system.

00:09:21.110 --> 00:09:27.800
If a candidate isn't talking about restructuring
the economic system, changing the relationship

00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:33.670
of humans to work, dealing with inequality
in a serious way, dealing with the economic

00:09:33.670 --> 00:09:39.500
activities that are not valued at all by our
current economy.

00:09:39.500 --> 00:09:44.540
Uh, and then also by the way, dealing with
activities that are profitable for lots of

00:09:44.540 --> 00:09:46.730
people, but actually aren't economically valuable.

00:09:46.730 --> 00:09:52.200
If you look at a ton of what the financial
worlds derivative instruments and speculation

00:09:52.200 --> 00:10:00.190
do, they can't actually be taken seriously
as drivers of economic value in a longterm

00:10:00.190 --> 00:10:01.190
way.

00:10:01.190 --> 00:10:04.830
A lot of that stuff that's going on on Wall
Street merely serves to enrich people without

00:10:04.830 --> 00:10:06.920
actually providing any stimulus to the economy.

00:10:06.920 --> 00:10:10.740
So if politicians aren't talking about that
stuff, if they're talking merely about, I'll

00:10:10.740 --> 00:10:16.200
bring back, uh, of, of manufacturing jobs
or whatever else, I don't take them seriously

00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:18.710
and you shouldn't take them seriously either.

00:10:18.710 --> 00:10:19.710
Let me know what you think.

00:10:19.710 --> 00:10:20.720
I'm on Twitter at the pacman.

00:10:20.720 --> 00:10:20.890
The show is on Twitter at David Pakman.

